\section*{An isolated lightest particle in the continuum O(3) spin field}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The continuum $O(3)$ spin field from the companion construction is claimed to have a lowest spectral mass represented by a positive atom, separated from further support.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Let $\phi=(\phi^1,\phi^2,\phi^3)$ be the Hermitian continuum spin field constructed from the two-dimensional nearest-neighbour $O(3)$ lattice model, using the fixed spacing and normalization of the companion construction. Its reconstructed spacetime has dimension $1+1$. With $\Delta_+(x;\mu^2)$ the positive-frequency scalar two-point distribution of mass $\mu$, write its spectral representation as
\[
 W_2^{ij}(x)=\delta_{ij}\int_{[0,\infty)}\Delta_+(x;\mu^2)\,\rho(d\mu^2).
\]
The new assertion identifies constants $m_1,Z,\delta>0$ and a positive measure $\rho_{\mathrm{rest}}$ such that
\[
 \rho=Z\delta_{m_1^2}+\rho_{\mathrm{rest}},\qquad
 \operatorname{supp}\rho_{\mathrm{rest}}\subset[(m_1+\delta)^2,\infty).
\]
Thus the lightest mass coupled to the spin field is an isolated atom, rather than merely a positive lower spectral edge.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Uniform finite-volume bounds from the massive construction are applied to the vector two-point spectral measure. Transfer and spectral comparisons distinguish a true isolated mass contribution from a merely positive continuous threshold, then preserve its positive residue in the continuum limit.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The continuum $O(3)$ spin field from the companion construction is claimed to have a lowest spectral mass represented by a positive atom, separated from further support. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 215, the selected theorem gives exponential finite-free-graph $O(n)$ spin decay for $n\geq3$ and bounded ferromagnetic edge strengths. Plane limits, full $O(4)$ spectral gaps, and continuum $O(3)$ results are beyond that entry. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

